/*! \page variance_clock Variance clock: fixed-snapshot runtime

`VarianceClock` integrates a supplied, market-specific variance-rate schedule.
Its unit is **weighted days**, not annualized volatility or price variance.
It does not change the existing `VarianceCalendar::weight` API, which linearly
interpolates point weights and applies category multipliers. A clock profile
instead supplies the complete constant rate for each local five-minute slot.
The cumulative clock is consequently linear *within* a slot, without
interpolating the rates at its boundaries.

## Inputs and time

Construct `VarianceClockTables`, then pass it to
`makeVarianceClockSnapshot(std::move(tables))`. Each profile contains 288
nonnegative finite binary64 weights and a calibration-as-of instant. Local
dates select profiles; explicit UTC intervals supply offsets and repeated-time
fold markers. The caller supplies these market and time-zone mappings: the
clock neither infers holidays and half days nor consults a time-zone database.
Offsets must be divisible by 300 seconds.

`VarianceClockInstant` represents POSIX seconds and a nonnegative nanosecond
fraction. Days have 86,400 POSIX seconds and leap seconds are not represented.
Use `tryCreate` to construct a valid instant; the default is invalid.
Coverage is a nonempty `[start, end)` interval, and both endpoints are valid
query instants. Endpoints and transitions can fall inside slots. Each actual
elapsed interval is counted through spring-forward and fall-back transitions.
Construction rejects invalid mappings and rates, and conservatively rejects
`maxWeight * coverageDays > DBL_MAX / 4`.

## Validated bundle loading

`loadVarianceClockBundle(source)` validates the fixed six-file manifest and CSV
bundle, verifies each CSV's SHA-256 against the manifest, and returns a complete
`VarianceClockSnapshot` with structured warnings and provenance. The
`loadVarianceClockBundleFromDirectory(nativeDirectory)` wrapper reads a native
directory; it must be used on a control path. Both APIs default to
`VarianceClockLoadMode::Strict`. Loading returns a candidate snapshot; activation
remains an explicit caller operation. A failure returns no partial result and
cannot mutate an active `VarianceClock`.

Strict mode sets provenance `availableFrom` to `generated_at_utc` and requires
`generated_at_utc < coverage_end_utc`. A regenerated historical bundle can
therefore load in Strict mode when it still has future coverage, but an earlier
valuation returns `BeforeCalibration`. An explicitly requested
`VarianceClockLoadMode::Reconstruction` omits `generated_at_utc` from the
availability maximum; it still validates all other evidence and cutoff rules.
Reconstruction records `reconstructed=true` and the chosen mode in returned
provenance only; neither is stored in the snapshot or `VarianceClock`.
It can load a purely historical bundle when its evidence and remaining checks
are valid.

The loader reads and retains each input once, in manifest-then-fixed-CSV order,
hashes the captured CSV bytes, and parses those same bytes. It enforces per-file
and aggregate byte limits, strict CSV/JSON schemas, cross-file evidence
relationships, and the load-memory budget. `VarianceClockBundleWarning` carries
the derived warning code, severity, profile, target group, minute, and evidence
IDs; provenance carries the snapshot ID, validated evidence IDs, availability,
and load mode. SHA-256 establishes byte equality with the manifest, not publisher
authenticity or the truth/completeness of the underlying market data. The query
path continues to perform no file I/O.

## Queries and numerical contract

Construct a fixed owner with the snapshot, capture a `VarianceClockView`, and
prepare a `VarianceClockExpiryToken` for each expiry. A token stores the expiry
position and generation identity, not a rounded global cumulative value.
Reuse the captured view and tokens for queries:

- `view.remaining(t, token)` integrates directly over `[t, T)`. It is
  mathematically `v(T) - v(t)`, but does **not** subtract rounded cumulative
  values. It sums nonnegative partial slots and balanced range-tree nodes.
- `view.cumulativeFromCoverageStart(t)` returns the clock from coverage start.
  Subtracting two such rounded results does not have the error guarantee of
  `remaining()`.
- `view.coverageStart()` and `view.coverageEnd()` return invalid instants for
  an invalid view. A failed `prepareExpiry` leaves its output token unchanged.

For the default IEEE-754 binary64 configuration, the interval acceptance
budget against an independent reference of at least 50 decimal digits is
`abs(actual - reference) <= 1e-12 + 1e-10 * abs(reference)` weighted days.
This is numerical error, not a bound on calibration or market-model error.
Rate contributions are evaluated as `weight * (duration / 86400)` to avoid
an avoidable intermediate overflow.

For `remaining`, status precedence is `InvalidView`, `InvalidToken`,
`StaleToken`, `InvalidInstant`, `OutOfCoverage`, `Expired`,
`BeforeCalibration`, then `Ok`. A covered `t >= T` returns `Expired` without
checking calibration. A nonempty interval requires the snapshot
`availableFrom` and every used profile's calibration-as-of to be no later than
the valuation instant, including profiles with zero rate. Cumulative queries
apply the same rule over `[start, t)`. An empty cumulative interval is `Ok`.
All non-`Ok` results have `weightedDays == 0.0`; an eligible zero-rate interval
is `Ok` with zero. `varianceClockAsReal` separately converts an `Ok` or
`Expired` result and rejects other statuses. Construction errors use QuantLib
error macros; query methods instead return statuses and are `noexcept`.

## Lifetime and scope

Views and tokens retain their immutable activation generation. Activation is
mutex-guarded; a new capture observes either the complete old generation or the
complete new one. An old view remains paired with its old token, while using
that token with a newly captured view returns `StaleToken`, including after an
A-to-B-to-equivalent-A sequence. The owner retains each replaced generation
before publishing its successor, so releasing a view or token cannot destroy
retired snapshot storage on that reader thread. The retired list grows until
`reclaim()` is called on a control thread; it removes generations owned only by
the manager and destroys them after dropping the mutex. The list can therefore
grow without bound if reclamation is not called.

`shutdown()` returns `false` without changing the active state while any view or
token retains a generation. Once handles are released, successful shutdown
destroys all owned snapshots on the control thread, makes capture return an
invalid view, and makes future activation fail. Shutdown is terminal. Destroy
views and tokens before destroying the owner: a violation aborts in debug
builds. In release builds, surviving handles retain and eventually destroy
their generation, outside any off-query reclamation guarantee. Capture,
activation, reclamation, and shutdown must not race with owner destruction.

The numeric query implementation performs no intended clock-owned allocation
or file I/O. Snapshot construction and activation are control-path operations
and can allocate. Bundle loading, hashing, CSV/JSON parsing, historical
calibration, and source provenance are outside this implementation. A supplied
availability cutoff does not itself prove source authenticity or point-in-time
provenance.

*/
